From e77fb6dd3d045809399e5cbf63d456b8e01fb4d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: venetanji <giovanni.lion@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 13:56:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Editable sketches: the code of a live sketch runs in place,
 and sliders for its numbers

A code_slide with a sketch is editable in the html deck: the code panel is an editor
(the exercise box, coloured as JavaScript) and Run re-runs the sketch on the right with
whatever is in it, so a student changes a number and sees the picture change without
leaving the slide. Reset brings the slide's code back; ctrl+enter runs; what is typed is
kept in localStorage per deck and re-run when the frame reloads; edit=False keeps the
panel static. The pptx, the png previews and the PDF keep the static panel and the still.

The deck never evaluates the code itself. The sketch page now runs its code through
runSketch(), which also takes new code by postMessage: it removes the p5 instance,
evaluates the code (plus extra) in one function scope so top-level lets can be declared
again, installs the p5 hooks it defined and starts p5 again; a syntax error, an error in
setup() or anything thrown later is posted back into the box. The code and the extra
travel into the page as string literals.

In a sketch page createSlider(min, max, value, step, label) is p5's slider plus a label and
its value in a bar under the canvas, and moving it redraws a noLoop() sketch; the fifth
argument is ours and the p5 web editor ignores it.

sketch.test.js types into the editor in a real browser (run, a syntax error, reset,
the saved code after a reload, the keyboard, print, the slider bar); sketch.test.py
covers the layout and the markup. The JS builtin list gains the p5 names the sketches use.

